meta {
  name: Deactivate User
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

delete {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{activeUserId}}
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("reports what went back to the unassigned queue", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("ticketsUnassigned");
    expect(body).to.have.property("rotationsDeactivated");
  });
}

docs {
  # DELETE /users/:userId

  Remove an employee from the agency. **Deactivates, never deletes** — contrast
  `DELETE /users/:userId/invite`, which really does delete because a pending
  invite owns no records. An active user is referenced by 31 `ref: 'User'` fields
  across 16 collections (two of them `required`), so removing the row would point
  every "produced by" column at a missing id.

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:users:write`.

  ## Access is revoked immediately, not at token expiry
  `AccessResolverService` re-resolves from Mongo on every request and returns
  nothing for an inactive user, so an already-issued JWT stops working on the
  removed user's very next call. Any live `inviteToken` / `passwordResetToken` is
  cleared at the same time — a credential must not outlive access.

  ## Their open work is released
  - Non-terminal service tickets are unassigned (`assignedUserId: null`) and the
    denormalized `assignedRep` is cleared. Resolved and closed tickets are left
    alone — those are history, not outstanding work.
  - Their CRM rotation entries are switched off, so the round-robin stops handing
    them new tickets. `CrmAssignmentService` builds its pool from
    `activeForProducer` and never checks whether the user still exists, so
    without this the work would leak straight back.

  **Attribution is never touched.** `deals.producerId`, `leads.producerId` and
  `quoteRecaps.producerId` record who did the work and stay exactly as they are.

  **Response `200`** — `{ ticketsUnassigned, rotationsDeactivated }`.

  **Errors**
  - `400` — you cannot remove your own account (it would be unrecoverable).
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:users:write`.
  - `404` — no such user in this agency, or the target is a platform admin.
  - `409` — already removed; **or** the target is a pending invite, in which case
    use `DELETE /users/:userId/invite` instead.

  ## ⚠ Running this against the demo tenant has a side effect
  **Reactivate User** (next) restores access, but the released work is
  deliberately *not* restored — those tickets may have been picked up by someone
  else in the meantime. A `bru run` therefore leaves the demo producer's open
  tickets unassigned. Restore with
  `npm run api:seed:demo:dev -- --fresh` if you need the Producer Dashboard's
  demo data intact.
}
